    "result_text": "All imports remain in use (method signatures reference `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`), compilation is clean, and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary of how I addressed the review\n\n**Finding 1 (validation logic change) \u2014 agreed, relocated the fix.**\nI reverted the `!isBlank(request.getCity())` guard in `ValidateOwnerFields.java` so the validation rules are untouched. The missing-city \u2192 400 path still can't be a 500, though, because the real defect was a latent NPE: `OwnerRegion.regionOf(null)` crashed on `Map.of().getOrDefault(null, \u2026)`. I fixed that at its source by making `regionOf` return `UNKNOWN` for a null city. That's a null-safety bug fix in the domain helper, not a validation-rule change \u2014 which is exactly the separation the reviewer wanted, while keeping the required acceptance behaviour (missing city rejects with 400).\n\n**Finding 2 (status specified twice) \u2014 agreed, fixed.**\nI promoted the \"build a problem response\" logic fully into `ProblemDetails`:\n- `responseEntity(ex, status, detail)` \u2014 builds the body and wraps it in a `ResponseEntity` with the same status, so the status is named once at the call site.\n- `asResponse(detail)` \u2014 for handlers that attach extra members first; it takes the status straight from the built `ProblemDetail`, so there's still a single source of truth.\n\n(I didn't use `ResponseEntity.of(ProblemDetail)` \u2014 in Spring 7 it returns a `HeadersBuilder`, not a bodied `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, so it doesn't fit cleanly.) The three near-identical owner handlers now call `responseEntity(...)`; `OwnerFieldsInvalid` (which adds `errors`) uses `asResponse(...)`. I also migrated the pre-existing escalation handlers (`DataIntegrity`, `General`, `Forbidden`, `Validation`) to the same two helpers, so the package the helper lives in doesn't end up with two competing patterns.\n\n**Finding 3 (hardcoded detail in `OwnerFieldsInvalid`) \u2014 confirmed intentional, left as is.**\nIt's deliberate: `OwnerFieldsInvalidException.getMessage()` is the internal `\"Missing or blank owner fields: [city]\"`, so the handler sends a stable user-facing `detail` and carries the field names in the `errors` extension member instead. The other three handlers' exception messages are already user-facing, so they pass `ex.getMessage()`.\n\nFull suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures.",
